{{Japanese name|Fujio}}

{{nihongo|'''Hideaki Fujio'''|藤尾 秀秋|Fujio Hideaki}} (8 March 1928 – 5 September 1999) was a Sanese politician who served as the 40th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from October to November 1990. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), Fujio attempted to salvage the party's reputation after the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] but was paralyzed by the emerging economic crisis and collapsed when coalition partners withdrew support. His 50-day premiership was one of the shortest in Sanese history.

== Early life ==
Fujio was born in [[Kochi Prefecture]] in 1928, the son of a fisherman who had been active in the cooperative movement. His rural, working-class background was unusual for an LDP leader, and he frequently cited his origins as evidence of his independence from the party's urban-centered establishment.

He attended [[Okami University]] on a scholarship, studying law while working part-time to support himself. After graduating in 1955, he entered the private sector, working for a regional bank before entering politics. His financial background gave him expertise in banking regulation and monetary policy, areas that would become central to his brief premiership.

== Political career ==
Fujio was first elected to the House of Commoners in 1967, representing [[Kochi 1st District]]. He built his political career on a platform of fiscal responsibility and anti-corruption reform, positioning himself as a "clean" alternative to the faction-ridden LDP mainstream. His reputation for integrity made him a valuable asset when the party needed to restore public trust.

During the [[Go-go 8090]] era, Fujio served as Chairman of the Budget Committee and later as Minister of Health and Welfare. His tenure in these positions was unremarkable but competent, and he avoided the entanglements with the construction industry that would prove fatal to many of his colleagues.

When the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] forced [[Kentaro Tokuyama]]'s resignation, the LDP turned to Fujio as a "clean" candidate who could restore public confidence. His selection was a desperate gamble—a man known for his integrity given the impossible task of saving a party drowning in corruption.

== Premiership (1990) ==
=== Attempting to salvage the LDP ===
[[File:Fujio LDP Factional Crisis 1990.png|thumb|right|200px|The LDP factional crisis that paralyzed Fujio's reform agenda]]
Fujio took office on 7 October 1990 with a clear mandate: clean house, restore public trust, and stabilize the government. He immediately announced a package of anti-corruption measures, including stricter financial disclosure requirements, the dissolution of factional fundraising operations, and the appointment of a special prosecutor to investigate the Miyazaki scandal.

However, Fujio's reform agenda was met with fierce resistance from within his own party. The LDP's faction leaders, many of whom had their own connections to the corrupt networks exposed by the scandal, viewed Fujio's reforms as an existential threat. They obstructed his legislative agenda, blocked his appointments, and leaked damaging information to the press.

=== The emerging economic crisis ===
Compounding Fujio's political difficulties was the emerging economic crisis. The [[Go-go 8090]] asset bubble, which had been inflated by the financial deregulation of the previous decade, was beginning to show signs of strain. The Bank of Okaiken had raised interest rates in early 1990 in an attempt to cool the speculative excess, and the resulting credit contraction was beginning to ripple through the economy. Property prices were falling, corporate profits were declining, and the first signs of a banking crisis were emerging.

Fujio's government was paralyzed by the twin crises of corruption and economic contraction. His attempts to pass an economic stimulus package were blocked by opposition parties and dissident LDP factions. His efforts to negotiate a coalition with the [[Okaiken Shintō|Okaiken New Party]] collapsed when the ONP demanded the resignation of several LDP cabinet ministers implicated in the scandal.

=== Collapse ===
On 26 November 1990, after just 50 days in office, Fujio's government collapsed when coalition partners withdrew support. He lost a vote of no-confidence in the House of Commoners, triggering the 1990 general election. The LDP suffered a devastating defeat, losing its majority for the first time in decades.

Fujio was succeeded by [[Shigehara Honda]] of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], who became the first non-LDP prime minister since the party's founding in 1977.

=== Domestic policy ===
Despite the brevity of his tenure, the Fujio government introduced several significant domestic policy initiatives. The government's anti-corruption package, announced on 15 October 1990, included the Political Funds Control Act Amendment, which imposed stricter financial disclosure requirements on political parties and their affiliated organizations, and the Public Procurement Transparency Act, which established competitive bidding requirements for all government contracts above a specified threshold. Both measures were blocked by the LDP's faction leaders, who viewed them as a threat to the patronage networks that sustained their political power.

On the economic front, the Fujio government attempted to address the emerging banking crisis through the Financial Stability Act of 1990, which would have authorized the government to inject public funds into failing banks and to establish a resolution authority for the orderly wind-down of insolvent financial institutions. The Act was a response to the growing problem of non-performing loans, which were threatening to destabilize the entire financial system. However, the Act was blocked by opposition parties and dissident LDP factions, who argued that the use of public funds to rescue private banks was unacceptable.

The government also introduced the Emergency Employment Measures Act of 1990, which provided funding for public works projects and job training programs to address the rising unemployment caused by the economic downturn. The Act was a modest measure, but it represented the government's recognition that the economic crisis was producing real hardship for ordinary citizens.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Fujio government's foreign policy was largely overshadowed by the domestic political crisis, but the government did pursue several initiatives. The government continued Okaiken's active participation in the [[Adonian Community]], supporting the organization's efforts to coordinate a response to the growing global economic slowdown. The government also participated in the Adonian Community Economic Summit of November 1990, which produced a joint statement committing member states to avoid protectionist measures and to coordinate monetary policy.

In the Shansian relationship, the Fujio government maintained the existing diplomatic framework, but the territorial disputes over the northern islands continued to simmer. The government authorized the renewal of the Shansian-Sanese Cultural Exchange Program, which had been established under previous administrations, but it resisted Shansian demands for the return of the northern territories, which had been under international administration since the Armistice of Okami.

The government also sought to strengthen Okaiken's economic ties with the Shendan states, dispatching a trade mission to negotiate bilateral investment agreements with several Shendan nations. The Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative, which had been launched under the Tokuyama government, was continued, and the government authorized increased funding for Shendan infrastructure projects.

== Legacy ==
Fujio's premiership is remembered as a tragic footnote in the LDP's decline. A man of genuine integrity, he was given an impossible task and failed—not through any fault of his own, but because the party he led was too corrupt to be saved and the economy too fragile to be stabilized.

His brief tenure demonstrated the depth of the crisis facing the LDP and the post-war political order. The combination of systemic corruption, economic collapse, and political paralysis that defined his premiership would continue to plague Sanese politics for the next several years, producing a rapid succession of short-lived governments.

Fujio retired from politics after the 1990 election defeat. He published a memoir in 2000, ''Fifty Days'', which provided a candid account of his premiership and the internal dynamics of the LDP during its crisis. He died in [[Okami]] in 1999 at the age of 71.

